    {
      "type": "grammar",
      "heading": "Gender in Punjabi",
      "body": "Every Punjabi noun is masculine or feminine. ਨਾਮ (naan, name) is masculine, so \"my\" becomes ਮੇਰਾ (mera). For a feminine noun like ਖੁਸ਼ੀ (khushi), \"my\" would be ਮੇਰੀ (meri).\n\nThe same rule applies to \"your\": ਤੁਹਾਡਾ (tuhada) before a masculine noun, ਤੁਹਾਡੀ (tuhadi) before a feminine one. You will feel this pattern everywhere — Punjabi words constantly adjust to the gender of the noun they describe."
    },
    {
      "type": "whyThisWay",
      "heading": "Why is it said this way?",
      "body": "\"Tuhada naan ki hai?\" is literally \"your name what is?\". The verb ਹੈ (hai) sits at the end, and the question word ਕੀ (ki) sits just before it.\n\nNotice also that Punjabi uses postpositions where English uses prepositions: English says \"from London\", Punjabi says \"London ton\" — the marker comes after the place, not before. You saw the same pattern in \"kithon\" (where + from squashed into one word)."
    },
    {
